WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.560
In a recent video we discussed

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.049
polynomial equations, or functions;

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:06.529
basically anything that looks like this,

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:10.740
x raised to a power. We explained if you

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.620
graph these things the solution or the

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.949
root or the zero to that polynomial can

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:19.529
be easily seen as the point or points

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:22.760
where the plot crosses the x axis.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.109
Professor Eisenbud also showed us that

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:27.390
an equation to an odd power must have

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.929
extreme points down here and up here,

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:33.270
meaning at some point it must cross the

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.329
x axis. So all polynomials to an odd

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:39.660
degree have at least one root among the

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.180
real numbers, maybe more if we get a

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.360
little kink like this. Great! But what

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.820
about even degreed polynomials? For

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.510
example, this one; well that's never going

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.480
to cross the x-axis. I mean, are there

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.039
polynomials for which there are no

00:00:56.039 --> 00:00:59.190
solutions? Well strap yourselves 
in because

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.170
thinking about that is leading us

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:02.609
towards one of the most important

00:01:02.609 --> 00:01:05.820
theorems in all of mathematics. 
Let's do it.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.830
So I'm going to start with that equation,

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.960
that equation of even degree that didn't

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.409
have any roots. f of x equals x squared

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:20.110
plus 1.
- (And that's not lost cause?)

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.810
That is not a lost cause. People probably

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.180
have heard of the square root of -1,
there's a

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.890
very famous mathematician, Sir Michael

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.289
Atiyah, who says that the square root of

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.590
-1, i, is the most important single

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.360
invention in all of mathematics.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.470
It certainly solves this equation

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:39.070
because if I claim that I have i equals

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.520
square root of -1 then by definition i

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.979
is a root of that polynomial. 
- (But there)

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.860
(is no i on here, we haven't got it.)
- We haven't

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.110
got an i so we have to find out where i

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.009
lives. And by the time of Gauss in 1800 it

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:56.709
was pretty well understood that you

00:01:56.709 --> 00:01:59.289
should think of a plane of numbers

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:03.009
called the complex plane. And if you put

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:04.990
in axes there will be a real axis and a

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:07.450
complex. And 1 will be in its

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.360
comfortable place there, and i will be

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.519
here. And you can do ordinary arithmetic

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:15.910
on these things, you write some number -

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.190
people usually use z for some reason for

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.489
complex numbers - so z would be a certain

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:24.040
real part a and a certain imaginary part,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.880
maybe b. And then I would write z equals

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.450
a the real part plus bi the imaginary

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.730
part. And complex numbers, you multiply

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.380
them just the way you think. If I want to

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:41.019
multiply 1 plus 2i times 2 minus i let's

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:43.390
say, then I just multiply them in parts

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.859
just as you would an algebraic

00:02:44.859 --> 00:02:47.019
expression if i were x. You can do

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:50.170
arithmetic, so I can plug any complex

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.060
number, the things in this plane are

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.950
called complex numbers things like z, I

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:56.049
can just plug them into any polynomial

00:02:56.049 --> 00:02:58.390
and I get another complex number because

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.280
I can multiply them and I can add them

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.810
and do all the things that I have to do.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.510
I can also divide them and they satisfy

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.120
all the comfortable laws of arithmetic

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.450
like commutativity, a times b is b

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:12.970
times a and all those things. So I can-

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.370
just comfortable arithmetic. And I can

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.830
use them in polynomials. If I do this I

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.739
might want to separate the source of the

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:23.019
polynomial, the place where the x's live,

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:25.450
with the values, the place where the f of

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.340
x's live. So I'm going to use a red

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.590
plane for that one. And now if I draw it

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:37.000
like this, so here's the again the 
real axis

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.790
and the imaginary axis. It's call the

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.440
imaginary axis, not because it isn't

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.420
there, it's just as real as the real axis,

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.120
but the numbers on it are the pure

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.099
imaginary numbers, the ones without any

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:50.560
real part. And then I can do things like

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.540
squaring the number, I can for example, I

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.840
could square i. And I would record the

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.730
value of the square over here; so for

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.760
instance if my polynomial f - let's use

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.459
green for the polynomial - so if I have f

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:08.980
of a blue x is a red x squared for

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:13.989
instance, then f of i would be i squared,

00:04:13.989 --> 00:04:16.930
which by definition is minus 1. So I

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.570
would I would say that f sort of takes

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.300
this point on the real axis over here,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.630
here's here's f the green line, over to

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.450
the minus 1 point there. So I think of

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.080
f as taking each point in this plane to

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.030
some point in that plane. So if I want to

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:35.500
think about the roots of a polynomial

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.440
now, a polynomial is one of these things

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.330
that takes a point here to a point here,

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:43.000
and the roots are the points in the blue

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.160
plane that it takes to the zero point in

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.110
the red plane.
- (Right in the middle there)

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.610
(in the bullseye?)
- Right in the middle right, here's

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.430
zero. That's our goal. Here's the

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:55.720
Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: it says,

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.720
this is due to Gauss, again I like to

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.660
write the word theorem. About 1800

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.900
I forget the exact date. 
- (He was pretty)

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.729
(handy that Gauss guy wasn't he?) 
- He was

00:05:06.729 --> 00:05:09.970
pretty good, yeah. People always like to

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:14.560
say, well, he's no Gauss but that's not a

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.110
not much of an insult. Anyway,

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:18.960
the theorem says that

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.570
any polynomial with complex coefficients -

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:27.090
can I spell coefficients? Yes. - having

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.759
degree d, that is the polynomial not the

00:05:29.759 --> 00:05:32.639
coefficients, has at least one complex

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:35.280
root but actually it follows from that

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:38.310
that it has d complex roots computed

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.500
with multiplicity, but let's just say has

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.130
at least one complex root, then we don't

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:46.380
have to talk about multiplicities. And

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:48.270
this is the Fundamental Theorem of

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.360
Algebra because it's the basic

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.240
connection between algebra and geometry.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.099
Roots are points somewhere, so they're

00:05:56.099 --> 00:05:59.849
geometric objects; and polynomials are

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:03.210
algebraic objects, so this is this is the

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.770
connection and makes all of algebraic

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.169
geometry work. It makes me happy because

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:09.360
I'm an algebraic geometer, it gives me

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.220
something interesting to do. 
- (So what)

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.650
(you're saying is there's no polynomial)

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.199
(that I could concoct that you wouldn't)

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:18.389
(be able to find roots for, as long as)

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:20.490
(you're allowed to have complex numbers)

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.510
(in your tool belt?)
- That's right except

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.030
for you're saying that I could find them.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.789
I'm going to show you they exist, I'm not

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:31.710
going to find them for you. If you want

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.260
to actually find the root then Newton's

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.389
method of fastest descent, things that-

00:06:36.389 --> 00:06:39.210
numerical methods are very powerful. But

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.389
it's hard to find roots of polynomials,

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:44.760
much harder than proving they exist. So

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:47.400
I'm just going to prove they exist. So

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.810
how can we do that? We can't do this

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.150
trick with lines but we can do a trick

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.759
that's a lot like it. And for that I have

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:55.830
to explain something about what happens

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.380
when you multiply complex numbers. So I

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.259
think of a complex number, here's my blue

00:07:01.259 --> 00:07:03.690
complex plane, and here's a complex

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.610
number. So I think of- I like to think

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.520
of it as a vector drawn from the origin.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.320
And if I think of the vector, the vector

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.659
really has a length, and then once I've

00:07:12.659 --> 00:07:14.250
told you the length, which is called the

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.130
absolute value of the complex number, so

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:19.919
z. So if you know the Pythagorean theorem

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:22.080
you know the length of this vector is

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.479
gotten by taking the square of the real

00:07:24.479 --> 00:07:26.759
part plus the square of the imaginary

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:29.220
part and taking the square root of that.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:34.650
So it's- so if z is a plus bi and the

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.810
absolute value of z,
I'll write it out, it's

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.360
the magnitude it's called, is the square

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.130
root of a squared plus b squared.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:44.310
Just Pythagorean theorem. Now there's

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:45.720
another thing that you need to determine

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.790
where that vector is, and that's the

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:51.030
angle, let's call it theta. Let's say we

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:54.960
knew that the magnitude was 1, then we

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.480
could compute the sine of theta and the

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.520
cosine of theta and if the magnitude

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.840
were 1, if this were of length 1, then the

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.750
sine which is the opposite side over the

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.610
hypotenuse would just be the opposite

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.470
side of the triangle which is the

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.960
imaginary part. And the real part would

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.980
be the cosine of the angle. So when I

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:19.290
multiply things, multiply complex numbers,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.780
I'm doing these tricks with the real and

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.090
imaginary parts, some- I should think of

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.680
that as something happening to sines and

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.100
cosines. And if you remember your high

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.600
school trigonometry, which I certainly

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.180
don't, then there's some addition law for

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.400
sines and cosines and it comes out to

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.160
saying this: if I take two complex

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.650
numbers z and w, here's w, and I take

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.720
their- the angles, let's call
that one psi;

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.660
then when I multiply them I multiply

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.760
their lengths, I multiply the magnitudes,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.410
and I add the angles. So I would take psi

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:01.200
and then I would add theta to it and I

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.490
would get something this way and the

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.230
length would be out here someplace, right?

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:08.280
So this is z times w. So it's really easy

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.340
to multiply complex numbers

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.780
geometrically, you just multiply the

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.030
lengths and add the angles. 
And that's some

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.830
formula which you can 
figure out, but it's-

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.450
I'm going to take it for granted.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.470
Now we're ready to go; and I want to take

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.839
an arbitrary polynomial - do you have more

00:09:24.839 --> 00:09:27.690
of this nice paper, Brady?
- (I do...)

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:34.180
And I want to think of this as going

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:40.810
from a blue complex plane, here's 1 and

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:47.950
here's i, to a red complex plane. 
- (So David)

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:51.280
(before, when we were doing the 
video before, when you)

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.040
(were doing this, we had two axes;)

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:57.070
(here you're representing-) 
- So each- this

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.960
this is one axis and this as the other

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.090
axis. If we wanted to make a real graph

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.980
like that we'd need to do it in four

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:05.020
dimensional space - and it's hard to

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:07.360
imagine four dimensional space. I want to

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.010
graph this function, I want to tell you

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.010
what happens when I apply x, and I want to

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:14.620
find the point here which- or at least

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.840
to convince you that there is a point, a

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:21.400
blue point, which will go to zero on the

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:24.040
red side. So that's what I want,

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:25.750
where is that point going to come from?

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:29.531
How am I going to find a point which
does that?

00:10:29.531 --> 00:10:31.480
(To prove one exists?) 
- To prove one

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:34.150
exists. So I start by scratching my head

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:35.740
and thinking, I don't know anything about

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.800
this polynomial just like before. But I

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.530
do know that the- I do know the leading

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.050
term, that's x to the n, and I know if x

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:46.420
is very big that's likely to be dominant.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.580
On the other hand if x is very small I

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.850
can do something too; 
if x is zero then the

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:54.570
value will be C. So I know that the

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:58.110
zero point over here in the blue plane

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:02.260
goes to C over in the red plane. So

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.540
here's a nice red C, let's say C is down

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.880
here. C is a complex number so it's just

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.690
someplace in that plane. And I know that

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:13.870
0 goes there. Now that's not terribly

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:15.820
helpful because I wanted to get a point

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:17.590
to go to 0 not to go to C.

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:21.520
So what can- what else do I know? I I know

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.640
that just as before, if I take x to have

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:27.760
enormously large magnitude, then x to

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.470
the n will have that magnitude to the

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.990
nth power, that'll be much much much

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.080
bigger than x to

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:37.440
the n minus 1. So for really large x's,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.980
x's way out in a big big circle

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:43.680
around this, those are the x's with big

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:47.880
magnitude. So this is magnitude of x much

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.910
bigger than 0. And now it's a circle

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:52.830
because it could have any argument, it

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:56.730
could have any angle. I know that x to

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.190
the n will be even bigger and I'm going to

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:01.830
shrink the scale on this red plane so

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.170
that I don't have to draw it off the- off

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:07.380
the table but it'll go to some huge

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:11.340
circle. Now, what will happen when I take

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.680
x and I take a walk with x? I think of it

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.720
as a dog on a leash and I lead it around

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.200
the circle? So we know what happens, the

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.060
magnitude - remember I'm pretending that

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.040
this polynomial is x to the n because

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.620
it's like x to the n - the magnitude
will stay

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.330
the magnitude of x to the n, the same

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.580
thing it was before. So we will walk

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:31.470
around this circle and the real

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.630
polynomial will stay close to the circle,

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.210
it'll sort of dance around like a dog

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:37.830
would on a leash after all.

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.660
Something else is happening though, as I

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:43.380
move x around this one moves n times as

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.630
fast because I'm multiplying the angles

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:48.630
by n. So it actually goes around n times.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.670
For the purposes of this proof it's

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.380
enough to know that it just goes

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.690
around but we'll see why the n is

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:57.960
important there too. So as I move around

